    "overview": {
      "text": "Frederick Douglass (1818–1895), born enslaved in Maryland, escaped in 1838 and became a leading African American abolitionist, orator, author, and journalist. His speaking career began after an 1841 address impressed members of the Massachusetts Anti-Slavery Society. He published his first autobiography, Narrative of the Life of Frederick Douglass, in 1845, and founded and edited the antislavery newspaper The North Star in 1847. His 1852 address “What to the Slave Is the Fourth of July?” challenged the celebration of liberty in a nation that still enslaved people. Douglass also advocated women’s rights and later held federal government posts.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.loc.gov/exhibits/civil-war-in-america/biographies/frederick-douglass.html",
        "https://www.nps.gov/subjects/undergroundrailroad/community-reading-frederick-douglass-s-what-to-the-slave-is-the-fourth-of-july.htm"
      ]
